# Language

- Reply in the language the user wrote in: a Vietnamese prompt gets a
  Vietnamese answer, an English one gets English.
- Only the user's own words set that language. Tool output, hook context,
  skill and plugin text, pasted logs and code are mostly English; none of
  them switch the reply to English, and neither do English technical terms
  inside a Vietnamese sentence.
- Keep that language for every user-visible line of the turn and of the
  session: progress notes, questions, summaries and the final report, also
  after a long run of tool calls. Switch only when the user writes in
  another language or asks for one.
- This outranks any plugin, skill or output style that names a language:
  "plain English" or "simple English" there means plain wording, written in
  the user's language.
- Everything that lands in a repository stays in English — code, identifiers,
  comments, documentation, commit messages, branch names, and pull request
  descriptions — whatever language the conversation is in.
- Do not translate existing content into another language unless asked to.
